ass="enc-snip">The Kashkadarya is a river in southern Uzbekistan that rises in the Pamir-Alay mountains and flows west past Shahrisabz and Qarshi into the desert.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashmir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kashmir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kashmir</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Kashmir is a mountainous region in the north of the Indian subcontinent, famed for its shawls.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashmiri-pandits"><span class="enc-name">Kashmiri Pandits</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Kashmiri Pandits are the Hindu Brahmin community of the Kashmir valley, long prominent as scholars, scribes and administrators.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashshu-nadin-ahi"><span class="enc-name">Kashshu-nadin-ahi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Kashshu-nadin-ahi was the last king of the Second Sealand Dynasty, ruling Babylon for about three years around 1007 to 1005 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kashta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kashta</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Kashta was a king of Kush in the eighth century BCE who extended Kushite authority into Upper Egypt.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashtiliash-iv"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kashtiliash-iv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kashtiliash IV</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Kashtiliash IV was a Kassite king of Babylon in the thirteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashu-chaidya"><span class="enc-name">Kashu Chaidya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Kashu Chaidya was a king of the Chedi people mentioned in the Rigveda, where a hymn praises him for generous gifts of cattle and camels to priests.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kasi-viswanathar-temple-tenkasi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kasi-viswanathar-temple-tenkasi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kasi Viswanathar Temple, Tenkasi</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Kasi Viswanathar Temple is a Hindu temple to Shiva at Tenkasi in Tamil Nadu, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kasimbazar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kasimbazar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kasimbazar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Kasimbazar is a town near Murshidabad in West Bengal, India, which in the 17th and 18th centuries was one of the main silk markets of Bengal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kasimov"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kasimov.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kasimov</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Kasimov is a town on the Oka river in Ryazan Oblast, Russia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kasivisvesvara-temple-lakkundi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kasivisvesvara-temple-lakkundi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kasivisvesvara Temple, Lakkundi</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Kasivisvesvara Temple is a Hindu temple at Lakkundi in Gadag district, Karnataka, built in the late Western Chalukya period, probably in the twelfth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kaskaskia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kaskaskia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Kaskaskia</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Kaskaskia were one of the main peoples of the Illinois Confederation, an Algonquian-speaking alliance of the upper Mississippi valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/kasogs"><span class="enc-name">Kasogs</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Kasogs was the name used in the medieval Rus' chronicles for the Circassians, the Adyghe peoples of the north-western Caucasus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/kasongo-luba"><span class="enc-name">Kasongo Luba</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Kasongo Luba was one of the two states into which the Luba kingdom divided after a succession civil war in the late nineteenth century.</span></a></li></ul>
